.TH WGET 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
wget \- retrieve files over HTTP, HTTPS, and FTP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wget
.RI [ options ]
.I url
.RI [ url ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wget
retrieves each
.I url
in turn and saves the document in a file.
This is the busybox applet: a small non-interactive downloader with a
fraction of the options of GNU Wget; see
.BR NOTES .
It speaks HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, and FTPS.
Messages and a progress meter go to standard error, and nothing goes to
standard output unless
.B "\-O \-"
asks for it.
.SS URLs
A URL is
.RI [ scheme ://][ user [: password ]@] host [: port ][/ path ].
The scheme is
.BR http ,
.BR https ,
.BR ftp ,
or
.BR ftps ,
in lowercase.
Any other scheme fails with
.BR "not an http or ftp url" .
Without a scheme, the URL is taken as http.
The default port is the standard one for the scheme.
.I host
is a name, an IPv4 address, or an IPv6 address in brackets.
Windows resolves a name.
When it has several addresses,
.B wget
tries only one: the first IPv4 address, or else the first address.
.PP
The host ends at the first
.BR / ,
.BR ? ,
or
.BR # .
The rest is the path, which
.B wget
sends to the server as given: it applies no percent-encoding, and a
query or fragment is part of the path.
.PP
.I user
and
.I password
are percent-decoded, and a
.B +
in them becomes a space.
Write a literal
.B +
as
.BR %2B .
.SS Output files
Without
.BR \-O ,
the file name is the last component of the URL path, the text after the
last
.B /
or
.BR \e ,
query included and not percent-decoded.
When that is empty, the name is
.IR index.html .
The file goes in the directory named by
.BR \-P ,
or else the current directory.
A redirection does not change the name.
.PP
Unless
.B \-c
is given,
.B wget
does not overwrite a file it names itself.
It fails with
.BI "can't open '" name "': File exists"
and does not try the remaining URLs.
It does not create directories either.
With
.BR \-O ,
the file is created or replaced.
.PP
The file is opened only after the server has accepted the request, so a
request that fails creates and truncates nothing.
If the connection ends early,
.B wget
retries as described under
.BR \-t ,
and what was received stays in the file, where
.B \-c
can continue it.
.SS HTTP
Each request is HTTP/1.1 with
.BR "Connection: close" ,
on a connection of its own.
The method is GET, or POST with
.B \-\-post\-data
or
.BR \-\-post\-file .
.B wget
sends
.BR Host ,
.B User\-Agent
(default
.BR Wget ),
.B Accept
(always
.BR */* ),
and
.B Connection
headers.
When the URL has a user, it also sends an
.B Authorization
header for Basic authentication with the first request, without waiting
for a challenge.
With
.B \-c
and a file that is not empty, it sends
.BR Range .
Headers from
.B \-\-header
follow, then, for a POST,
.B Content\-Type
and
.BR Content\-Length .
It sends no cookies and no
.BR Accept\-Encoding ,
and it does not decode
.BR Content\-Encoding .
.PP
The status decides what happens next:
.TP
.B 100
The response is skipped, and the one after it is used.
.TP
.BR 200 \- 204
The document follows.
.TP
.B 206
The document follows, but only if
.B \-c
asked for a range.
.TP
.BR 300 ", " 301 ", " 302 ", " 303 ", " 307 ", " 308
If there is a
.B Location
header, the request is repeated at that URL, at most 15 times: the 16th
redirection fails with
.BR "too many redirections" .
The method, body, and headers stay the same.
A location that begins with
.B /
is on the same server.
Any other location is parsed as a URL, so a relative one without the
leading slash is taken as a host name.
Without a
.B Location
header, the response is treated like a 200 and its body is saved.
.TP
Anything else
The command fails with
.BI "server returned error: " status-line \fR.
This includes 401: there is no way to answer a challenge.
.PP
The body is read for as many octets as
.B Content\-Length
says, or in chunks for
.BR "Transfer\-Encoding: chunked" ,
or else to the end of the connection.
Any other transfer encoding is an error.
A connection that closes before the octets that
.B Content\-Length
promised, or before the last chunk, is reported as
.BI "connection closed at byte " n\fR,
where
.I n
counts the octets received over that connection, and the download is
retried as described under
.BR \-t .
.PP
Lines from the server, in HTTP and FTP alike, are cut at their first
control character other than a tab, and tabs become spaces.
.SS FTP
The connection is passive.
The login is
.B anonymous
with the password
.BR busybox ,
or the user and password from the URL, with an empty password if the URL
has only a user.
Then
.B wget
sends
.B TYPE I
and
.B SIZE
(for the size, if the server knows it),
.B EPSV
(or else
.BR PASV ),
.B REST
(for
.BR \-c ),
and
.BR RETR .
The transfer must end with reply 226.
.PP
The path is the text after the first
.BR / ,
sent to the server as given.
It is relative to the login directory.
For an absolute path, double the slash, as in
.BR ftp://host//pub/file .
.PP
For
.BR ftps ,
TLS starts as soon as the control connection opens, on port 990, and the
data connection is encrypted too when the server accepts
.BR "PROT P" .
.B \-\-no\-check\-certificate
does not apply to it.
.SS HTTPS and FTPS
.B wget
runs
.BR ssl_client (1)
for each encrypted connection.
Windows does the TLS: it checks the certificate chain against the
Windows certificate store, and the certificate's names against the host
name.
A failed handshake is reported by
.B ssl_client
on standard error, followed by
.BR "wget: error getting response" .
.SS Messages
Unless
.B \-q
is given, standard error shows these lines for each URL:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
Connecting to \fIhost\fR (\fIaddress\fR)
saving to \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq
\fIprogress meter\fR
\(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq saved
.EE
.RE
.PP
.I host
is written as in the URL, with any port, or is the proxy's, and
.I address
includes the port.
Without a proxy, a redirection to a full URL prints another
.B Connecting to
line.
With
.BR "\-O \-" ,
the second and fourth lines are
.B writing to stdout
and
.BR "written to stdout" .
With
.BR \-\-spider ,
only the first line is printed, followed by
.BR "remote file exists" .
.PP
The progress meter has the file name, cut or padded to 20 columns, then
the percentage and a bar of asterisks, when the size is known, then the
amount received so far and the time remaining.
The bar is as wide as the console less 48 columns, and is left out when
that is 2 or less.
The amount counts any part of the file already present with
.BR \-c .
It is in octets up to 99999, and above that in units of 1024 with a
suffix of
.BR k ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
and so on.
The time is
.B H:MM:SS ETA
or, when it is not known,
.BR "\-\-:\-\-:\-\- ETA" ;
after 5 seconds without data it reads
.BR "\- stalled \-" .
On a console, the meter redraws one line about once a second.
When standard error is not a console, it writes a new line each time
instead.
.PP
The meter is not shown with
.BR \-q ,
or with
.B \-o
and a file.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may come before or after the URLs, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set, and short options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-cq .
The argument of a short option may be attached or be the next argument.
A long option takes its argument after an equals sign or as the next
argument, and any unique prefix of a long option is enough.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.B \-\-spider
Check that the URL can be retrieved, without saving it.
.B wget
makes the usual request, and on success prints
.B remote file exists
(unless
.B \-q
is given) and goes on to the next URL without reading the document.
Failures are those of a download.
Over FTP, the data connection is closed unread, and a final reply other
than 226 fails.
.TP
.BI "\-\-header " string
Add
.I string
to the request headers, after the standard ones and before
.B Content\-Type
and
.BR Content\-Length .
It is a line of the form
.IB name ": " value
and is sent as given.
This option may be repeated.
A header whose name is
.BR Host ,
.BR User\-Agent ,
.BR Range ,
.BR Content\-Type ,
.BR Accept ,
.BR Authorization ,
.BR Proxy\-Authorization ,
or
.BR Content\-Length ,
compared without regard to case and followed by a colon, replaces the
header
.B wget
would otherwise send.
All headers are sent again after a redirection, even to another host.
.TP
\fB\-U\fR \fIstring\fR, \fB\-\-user\-agent\fR=\fIstring\fR
Send
.I string
as the
.B User\-Agent
header.
The default is
.BR Wget .
.TP
.BI "\-\-post\-data " string
Send
.I string
as the body of a POST request.
It is sent as given, with
.B Content\-Type: application/x\-www\-form\-urlencoded
and
.BR Content\-Length ,
unless
.B \-\-header
gives them.
It may be given once.
.TP
.BI "\-\-post\-file " file
Send the contents of
.I file
as the body of a POST request, like
.BR \-\-post\-data .
A
.I file
of
.B \-
is standard input, and then a redirection or a second URL makes
.B wget
hang.
The file is read again for each request.
The body ends before the first NUL octet in the file.
.IP
Give
.B \-\-post\-file
once, and after
.BR \-\-post\-data ,
if both: the other order is an error, and in this one the file wins.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-check\-certificate
Do not validate the certificate of an https server.
Without it, validation is as described under
.BR "HTTPS and FTPS" .
It has no effect on ftps.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-continue
Continue an interrupted download.
If the output file exists, the rest of the document is requested, with
.B Range
for HTTP and
.B REST
for FTP, and appended.
If it does not exist, the download starts normally.
The existing part is not compared with the server's copy.
.IP
When the server will not resume (HTTP status 200 to 204, or a refused
.BR REST ),
the message
.B wget: restart failed
appears, and the whole document is written from the start of the file.
The file is cut to the new length only at the end, so an interrupted
restart leaves the rest of the old data in place.
Any other status, such as 416 from a server that sees a complete file,
is an error.
.IP
With
.BR "\-O \-" ,
.B \-c
is ignored.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Do not print the messages and progress meter described under
.BR Messages .
Errors, the
.B connection closed
and
.B restart failed
messages of
.B \-t
and
.BR \-c ,
and the output of
.B \-S
are still printed.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR \fIdir\fR, \fB\-\-directory\-prefix\fR=\fIdir\fR
Save files in
.I dir
instead of the current directory.
It must exist.
It is ignored with
.BR \-O .
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-server\-response
Print the server's replies on standard error.
For HTTP, each line is indented by 2 spaces.
The status line of each response is shown, then its headers, up to and
including the empty line that ends them.
For a redirection, the headers stop at
.BR Location ,
and for an error status, only the status line is shown.
For FTP, each reply line is shown, and each command that
.B wget
sends, as
.BI "\-\-> " command
followed by an empty line; that includes the password, in the
.B PASS
command.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fItries\fR, \fB\-\-tries\fR=\fItries\fR
Retry a download whose connection closes before the body is complete,
up to
.I tries
times after the first request.
The default is 20, and 0 means no limit.
.I tries
is a decimal number; anything else is an error.
Other failures, such as a refused connection or an error status, are
not retried.
.IP
Each retry connects again, to the URL that any redirections reached, and
asks for the rest of the document, with
.B Range
for HTTP and
.B REST
for FTP, as
.B \-c
does.
The rest is written after what was received.
When the server sends the whole document instead, the message
.B wget: restart failed
appears, and the document is written again from where it began in the
output file.
Standard output that is a pipe cannot be rewound, so there that ends
the command with an error.
When the tries run out,
.B wget
keeps what it received, without the
.B saved
message, and goes on to the next URL.
That is not a failure, so the exit status can still be 0.
.TP
.BI "\-T " sec
Not supported.
This build has no timeout feature, and
.B \-T
makes
.B wget
crash; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
\fB\-O\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-output\-document\fR=\fIfile\fR
Save the document in
.I file
instead of a name derived from the URL.
A
.I file
of
.B \-
is standard output.
.IP
The file is created, or replaced if it exists.
When there are several URLs, their documents are written to the file one
after another.
After each complete document the file is cut off at the end of what was
written.
That applies to standard output too, when it is a file, so a file opened
with
.B 1<>
loses the old data after the document, though one opened with
.B >>
does not.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR \fIlogfile\fR, \fB\-\-output\-file\fR=\fIlogfile\fR
Write messages to
.I logfile
instead of standard error, replacing the file if it exists.
It is opened before the first URL, and failing to open it is an error.
Everything else that
.B wget
would write to standard error goes there, including error messages and
the output of
.BR \-S ,
and the progress meter is not shown.
A
.I logfile
of
.B \-
leaves standard error alone.
.TP
\fB\-Y\fR \fIon/off\fR, \fB\-\-proxy\fR=\fIon/off\fR
A value of
.B off
makes
.B wget
ignore the proxy variables described under
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
Any other value, and the default, uses them.
.TP
.BI \-n arg
Accepted and ignored, as in
.BR \-nv ,
.BR \-nc ,
.BR \-nH ,
and
.BR \-np .
Any text may follow the
.BR n ,
and it must be attached.
With
.B \-nc
or
.BR \-\-no\-clobber ,
an existing file is still an error, as described under
.BR "Output files" ,
not skipped.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-cache ", " \-\-no\-clobber ", " \-\-no\-host\-directories
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-parent ", " \-\-no\-verbose ", " \-\-passive\-ftp
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.PP
Any other option, a missing option argument, an ambiguous long option, a
repeated
.B \-\-post\-data
or
.BR \-\-post\-file ,
.B \-\-post\-data
after
.BR \-\-post\-file ,
or no URL prints the usage on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The proxy variables are named in lowercase, but Windows ignores the case
of variable names, so
.B HTTP_PROXY
works too.
There is no
.BR https_proxy ,
and no
.BR no_proxy .
.TP
.B http_proxy
The proxy for http and https URLs, as a URL:
.RB [ http:// ][\c
.IR user [: password ]@] host [: port ].
Any path is ignored, and the port defaults to 80.
A proxy URL that begins with
.B https://
is reached over TLS, on port 443 by default.
When the variable is set and not empty, and
.B \-Y
is not
.BR off ,
.B wget
connects to the proxy and requests the full URL, as in
.BR "GET http://host/path" .
The method is always GET, even with
.B \-\-post\-data
or
.BR \-\-post\-file ,
whose body is still sent.
.I user
and
.I password
become a
.B Proxy\-Authorization
header.
.TP
.B ftp_proxy
The same, for ftp and ftps URLs.
The proxy is spoken to in HTTP, as with
.BR http_proxy .
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The console width used for the progress meter.
Without it, the width is that of the console window on standard output,
or else on standard error, or else 80.
A value that is not a number from 2 to 29999 means 80.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, and when the ANSI code page is
UTF-8, each non-ASCII octet of the file name in the progress meter is
shown as
.BR ? .
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first URL ends the options.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every URL was retrieved, or found with
.BR \-\-spider ,
or the usage was requested with
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, or any failure while handling a URL: a bad
address, a refused connection, an unusable URL, a refused file, an error
status from the server, or an error reading or writing.
The message is on standard error, prefixed with
.BR wget: .
.PP
The first failure ends the command: the remaining URLs are not
retrieved, and the files already saved stay.
A body still cut short after the retries of
.B \-t
is not a failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
Download a file into the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ wget https://example.com/kit.zip
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save it in another directory, or under another name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ wget \-P downloads https://example.com/kit.zip
$ wget \-O kit.zip \(aqhttps://example.com/dl?id=7\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print a document on standard output:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ wget \-qO\- https://example.com/version.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Continue an interrupted download:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ wget \-c https://example.com/big.iso
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test whether a URL exists:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ wget \-q \-\-spider http://localhost:8000/ && echo up
.EE
.RE
.PP
Send a form with a POST request and an extra header:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ wget \-qO\- \-\-header \(aqX\-Token: abc\(aq \e
>     \-\-post\-data \(aqa=1&b=2\(aq http://localhost:8000/form
.EE
.RE
.PP
Post a file from standard input:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ wget \-qO\- \-\-post\-file \- http://localhost:8000/ <data.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give up on a transfer after 60 seconds, since
.B wget
has no timeout:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ timeout 60 wget https://example.com/kit.zip
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU Wget:
.IP \(bu 2
Only the options above exist.
There is no recursion, no list of URLs read from a file, no
timestamping, no way to give a user and password except in the URL or an
.B Authorization
header, no cookies, and no rate limit.
.IP \(bu 2
Only a body cut short is retried, at once and without a wait, and
running out of tries is not an error.
.IP \(bu 2
An existing file is an error: it is not kept by saving the new one as
.IR name.1 .
.PP
There is no timeout: a stalled transfer waits forever, though Windows
gives up on a connection attempt that gets no answer.
The timeout feature of busybox is not built:
.B \-\-timeout
is an unknown option, and
.B \-T
with a number passes the option parser and then crashes with an access
violation.
Use
.BR timeout (1),
but for https it stops only
.BR wget :
the
.BR ssl_client (1)
process that
.B wget
started can keep running until the server sends something or closes
the connection.
That process holds the standard output and standard error of
.BR wget ,
so a command substitution or pipeline that reads them waits for it too.
.PP
TLS is Windows's Schannel, run through
.BR ssl_client (1),
which describes the protocols (TLS 1.0 through 1.2 here) and the
handshake error messages.
A server that needs TLS 1.3 cannot be reached.
The certificate is validated unless
.B \-\-no\-check\-certificate
is given.
An https URL sent through
.B http_proxy
is not tunneled: the proxy receives the request in clear text, as
.BR "GET https://host/path" .
.PP
Because of how
.BR ssl_client (1)
copies data, a large request body over https, such as a
.B \-\-post\-file
of 40000 octets, can stall before it is all sent, or reach the server
with earlier parts of the request repeated in place of later ones.
Over https, a response without
.B Content\-Length
makes
.B wget
hang when the server waits for the client to end the TLS session before
it closes the connection.
.PP
Credentials in the URL, and headers from
.BR \-\-header ,
are sent to the target of a redirection even when it is on another host.
.PP
A name derived from the URL must be a valid Windows file name.
A URL such as
.B http://host/get?id=7
fails with
.BR "can't open 'get?id=7': Invalid argument" .
Use
.B \-O
to name the file.
Non-ASCII octets in the path are used as they are, so a UTF-8 path gives
a UTF-8 file name when the ANSI code page is UTF-8; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Paths given to
.BR \-O ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-P ,
and
.B \-\-post\-file
may use forward or back slashes.
The file name is joined to the
.B \-P
directory with
.BR / ,
except after a bare drive:
.B \-P c:
saves to
.IR c:name ,
in the current directory of the drive.
Documents, posted files, and standard output are binary: octets pass
unchanged.
.PP
A URL with a query directly after the host, as in
.BR http://host?q=1 ,
loses the question mark and requests
.BR /q=1 .
Write
.B http://host/?q=1
instead.
.PP
When the host is an IP address, the
.B Connecting to
line shows
.B (null)
in the parentheses instead of the address.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR httpd (1),
.BR nc (1),
.BR ssl_client (1),
.BR timeout (1)
